From 058d94afc6ed53cf1fe0cec56f498425b299522b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: llying-001 <liyingli@amd.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:32:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] [TransferEngine] Add opt-in destination-local GPU write
 rails (#4540)

* feat(te): add opt-in destination-local GPU write rails

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* fix(te): print enable_dest_local_rail as true/false like the other flags

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>
Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>

* test(te): key destination-rail test GPUs by GPU_PREFIX

The test wrote cuda: locations, which topology does not treat as GPU memory
under HIP (hip:) or other vendors, so it failed on exactly the builds the
option targets. Locations are now written as gpu: and mapped to GPU_PREFIX.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* fix(te): select destination-hinted requests outside the device cache

BatchRdmaDeviceCache requires a fixed hint, while the destination hint follows
each request's target GPU. It only caches single-HCA segments today, so this is
latent, but a wider cache would silently ignore the hint. Requests with a
destination hint now select uncached. The docs spell out the sender-side
trade: the named HCA may not be local to the source GPU.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* ci: retrigger after the CTest runner was lost mid-build

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* fix(te): keep the destination rail across MRs and out of the unhinted shortcut

- destinationLocalHca() resolved the destination with one buffer lookup, so a
  WRITE crossing adjacent MRs of one GPU (a receiver splitting an allocation by
  MC_MAX_MR_SIZE) lost its hint. The first and last bytes are now resolved
  separately and the hint applies when both are on the same GPU.
- A destination-hinted NIC was stored as last_local_device_id, so a following
  unhinted request or slice on the same source MR reused it instead of the
  source-local NIC. Hinted selections no longer feed that shortcut.

Regression tests: a WRITE across two MRs of one GPU, and mixed batches in
rdma_transport_submit_task_test at request and slice level.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* test(te): give the destination-rail test buffers MR keys

Since #5185 device selection skips HCAs without valid keys for the buffer,
and a buffer with none selects nothing. Real descriptors carry per-HCA
keys; the test buffers now do too.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* ci: retrigger after a PyPI read timeout in the wheel smoke tests

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* refactor(te): pick the WRITE destination NIC through selectDevice()

Following review, build on Topology and selectDevice() instead of a
parallel pin table and lookup:

- Drop Topology::pinned_hca_ / resolvePinnedDevices(). The destination
  HCA is the one the peer's topology selects for the target GPU, so it
  follows preferred_hca. Hosts whose auto-discovered preferred sets tie
  several HCAs per GPU describe one HCA per GPU in MC_CUSTOM_TOPO_JSON.
- Replace destinationLocalHca() with selectDestinationNic(), which runs
  selectDevice() on the target segment with a per-MR cursor
  (last_remote_device_id) and returns the HCA name as a string_view into
  the peer's HCA list. A request whose destination no single remote MR
  covers skips request-level selection; its slices, cut at remote MR
  boundaries, select one by one. The local device cache is used as for
  any hinted request, and last_local_device_id is updated only for
  unhinted picks, which covers explicit nic_hint too.
- One knob, MC_RDMA_WRITE_PREFER_DEST_NIC, replacing
  MC_ENABLE_DEST_LOCAL_RAIL. It implies MC_ENABLE_DEST_DEVICE_AFFINITY
  so the peer answers on the same-named NIC; a worker reuses its last
  peer pick across slices, so the pairing covers every operation. It
  yields to MC_ENABLE_HCA_PEER_AFFINITY.

Tests cover the destination NIC for GPU and host memory, ranges no single
peer MR covers, the cursor keeping the peer pick for one MR, the config
knob, and in submitTransferTask the default-off path, no reuse of a
destination pick, and a WRITE crossing from one peer GPU into another.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* docs(te): say what MC_RDMA_WRITE_PREFER_DEST_NIC leaves to usual selection

The option implies destination affinity for every operation; what READs, writes into host memory, senders without a same-named HCA and retries keep is the sender's usual HCA choice.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

* fix(te): resolve MC_RDMA_WRITE_PREFER_DEST_NIC before the affinity clash

With MC_ENABLE_HCA_PEER_AFFINITY, MC_ENABLE_DEST_DEVICE_AFFINITY and MC_RDMA_WRITE_PREFER_DEST_NIC all set, the clash between the two affinities turned both off and the option then turned destination affinity back on. Decide the option first: next to HCA peer affinity it is ignored, and the existing clash applies unchanged, so all three set behave as the two affinities alone.

Signed-off-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>

